---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import parameters from '../../generated/parameters.json';
import registry from '../../generated/registry.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

interface Citation {
  statute: string;
  session_law: string | null;
  source_url: string | null;
  quote: string | null;
  verified: boolean;
  verified_date: string | null;
  verified_by: string | null;
}
interface Parameter {
  key: string;
  value: number | string | boolean | null;
  unit: string;
  description: string;
  citation: Citation;
  applies_to: string | null;
  contingent: boolean;
  is_law: boolean;
  structural_note: string | null;
  range: { low: number; high: number; basis: string } | null;
}
interface ParameterSet {
  file: string;
  fiscal_year: number;
  status: string;
  note: string | null;
  unverified_count: number;
  parameters: Parameter[];
}

const sets = parameters as ParameterSet[];
const counts = registry.counts as Record<string, number>;
---

<Base title="Methodology — Vermont School Budgets">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    What this site holds, how it computes, and what it refuses to do. Published with the same
    prominence as any result, because a result whose method is buried is not independently
    verifiable.
  </p>

  <h2>The registry</h2>
  <p>
    Everything keys off a canonical record of who exists, synced nightly from the Agency of
    Education's public data API and archived in full on every sync. It currently holds
    {counts['su']} supervisory unions, {counts['ud']} unified districts, {counts['school']}
    schools, {counts['town']} towns, {counts['independent']} independent schools,
    {counts['techcenter']} technical and vocational centres and {counts['academy']} academies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Entities have stable internal identifiers of our own, mapped to AOE's. AOE identifiers and
    names will churn as the 2029 reorganization proceeds; a URL cited in a committee packet has
    to keep resolving afterwards. Entities are never deleted — they close, with a date — so
    before-and-after comparisons across a reorganization remain possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two limits are worth stating plainly. The API publishes no opening dates, so an entity's
    "first recorded" date is the date our sync first observed it and is labelled as such, not as
    a founding date. And it publishes no successor relationships at all, so any statement that
    one district succeeded another is hand-entered from statute and carries a note saying so.
  </p>

  <h2>The budget schema</h2>
  <p>
    One record per district per fiscal year, deliberately coarse: about a dozen rollup categories
    aligned to the state's chart of accounts, not four hundred line items. Depth can be added
    later; it cannot easily be removed once a hundred documents have been extracted at the wrong
    grain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each record carries two different cuts of <em>the same dollars</em>. Expenditures slice
    spending by function — instruction, special education, district administration, school
    administration, operations, transportation, debt service. Personnel slices it by object —
    salaries, health insurance, other benefits, and full-time-equivalent headcount. The two
    describe one total and are never additive; validation rejects any record where staff costs
    exceed total expenditure, because that can only mean one of them was misread.
  </p>
  <p>
    Health insurance is broken out on its own because it is the cost driver most board arguments
    are actually about, and because it is the line that keeps growing when headcount does not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a district prints a figure that disagrees with our recomputation — per-pupil spending
    especially — both are kept and the disagreement is recorded. It is not reconciled away. The
    discrepancy is frequently the interesting part.
  </p>

  <h3>What a blank means</h3>
  <p>
    Every blank money field in the warehouse must be accounted for: either listed as not
    published by the source document, with the name of the person who confirmed that and the date
    they did, or flagged as a line that did not fit cleanly. Validation fails otherwise. This is
    what makes a blank mean "the district did not publish this" rather than "nobody looked" —
    without the rule, those two are the same value and the data cannot be trusted.
  </p>

  <h2>The engine</h2>
  <p>
    The formula engine is a small dependency-free library used both by the build (to precompute
    baselines) and by your browser (to run scenarios). Its output is not a number but a tree:
    every node carries its inputs, the operation applied, the result, an explanation in plain
    language, and the citation of every statutory parameter used. The walkthrough you see in the
    modeling tool is that tree rendered directly — the narration and the arithmetic are produced
    in the same call and cannot drift apart.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two properties are structural rather than matters of discipline. An unverified statutory
    parameter cannot produce a number: the node returns nothing, is marked unverified, and that
    status propagates to everything above it, so an unchecked weight cannot become a published
    figure four steps downstream. And "we have not verified this against statute" is a different
    status from "the district did not publish this" — the engine keeps them distinct all the way
    to the screen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="blanks">The four kinds of blank</h2>
  <p>
    A blank on this site always means one specific thing, and the site says which. There are
    four, they belong to different parties, and each implies a different remedy. Rendering any
    two of them identically would misattribute a gap to whoever is easiest to blame.
  </p>
  <dl>
    <dt><span class="tag unverified">unverified</span></dt>
    <dd>
      A statutory value has not been confirmed against current law. <strong>Our outstanding
      work.</strong> The remedy is that somebody reads the act.
    </dd>
    <dt><span class="tag missing-input">missing input</span></dt>
    <dd>
      The source document does not publish the figure. <strong>A fact about the
      document.</strong> Nobody here fixes it by working harder; it is recorded, with who
      confirmed the absence and when.
    </dd>
    <dt><span class="tag undetermined">undetermined</span></dt>
    <dd>
      The State has not made a decision that does not yet exist. <strong>Nobody's, yet.</strong>
      The Agency of Education has published no small or sparse by necessity determinations
      because the rules governing them are unwritten — showing that as a missing document would
      imply the Agency failed to publish something, which is false.
    </dd>
    <dt><span class="tag not-computable">not computable</span></dt>
    <dd>
      The question cannot be answered from public data at all: it needs a certification, a local
      model, or a demographic projection. <strong>Nobody's, ever.</strong> This is a correct
      final answer rather than a task, and a coverage view that painted it red would be
      misdescribing what finished looks like.
    </dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    Separately from all four, a figure may be <span class="tag contingent">contingent</span> —
    resting on legislation that has not been enacted. That qualifies a value rather than
    withholding one, and a figure can be both contingent and blank.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href={`${base}/methodology/small-sparse/`}>small and sparse schools methodology</a>
    covers the layer that needed the last two.
  </p>

  <h2>Parameters and citations</h2>
  <p>
    Weights, yields and thresholds are data, not code. When the Legislature changes something,
    which it has in most recent sessions, the change is an edit to a file like the one below and
    every calculation, citation and explanation updates with it.
  </p>

  {
    sets.map((set) => (
      <>
        <h3>
          FY{set.fiscal_year} <span class="tag">{set.status}</span>
        </h3>
        {set.unverified_count > 0 && (
          <div class="notice blocking">
            <strong>
              {set.unverified_count} of {set.parameters.length} parameters are unverified
            </strong>
            <p>
              No value below has been confirmed against current statute text, so the engine will
              not compute with any of them. The authoritative sources — the Legislature's statutes
              site and the Agency of Education — were not reachable from the environment this file
              was drafted in, and filling the values in from memory or from a secondary mirror
              would be precisely the failure this site exists to be the opposite of. Vermont's
              education funding statutes were amended by Act 127 of 2022 and Act 73 of 2025, so a
              remembered weight is quite likely to be a repealed one.
            </p>
          </div>
        )}
        <div class="scroll-x">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Parameter</th>
                <th scope="col">Value</th>
                <th scope="col">Citation</th>
                <th scope="col">Verified</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {set.parameters.map((p) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>{p.key}</code>
                    <br />
                    <span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:.88rem">{p.description}</span>
                  </td>
                  <td class="numeric">
                    {p.value === null ? <span class="tag missing-input">not set</span> : String(p.value)}
                    {p.contingent && <br />}
                    {p.contingent && <span class="tag contingent">contingent</span>}
                    {/* A proposal must never read as a rule anywhere it is shown. */}
                    {p.is_law === false && <br />}
                    {p.is_law === false && <span class="tag undetermined">proposed, not law</span>}
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    {p.citation.source_url ? (
                      <a href={p.citation.source_url} rel="noopener">{p.citation.statute}</a>
                    ) : (
                      p.citation.statute
                    )}
                    {p.citation.session_law && (
                      <>
                        <br />
                        <span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:.88rem">
                          {p.citation.session_law}
                        </span>
                      </>
                    )}
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    {p.citation.verified ? (
                      <span class="tag ok">{p.citation.verified_date}</span>
                    ) : (
                      <span class="tag unverified">not verified</span>
                    )}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <h2>The foundation formula</h2>
  <p>
    Act 73 of 2025 sets Vermont on a course toward a base-amount formula with a statewide rate,
    contingently effective for the 2028–29 reorganization. The Legislature has not set its
    parameters. Those parameters are therefore marked contingent and hold no value at all — not a
    placeholder, not an estimate. When there is a defensible basis for a range, such as published
    consultant recommendations, they will carry a range with that basis attached rather than a
    point estimate, and everything computed from them will be labelled contingent throughout.
  </p>
  <p>
    One structural point matters more than any number here: under a statewide rate, a district's
    own budget no longer determines its own rate. That is a different causal structure from the
    current yield system, not merely different arithmetic, and the tool says so where it applies.
  </p>

  <h2>Golden tests</h2>
  <p>
    Before any computed figure is published, the engine must reproduce the state's own published
    weighted membership and announced tax rates for a sample of districts, to within rounding.
    Those fixtures live in the repository and run in continuous integration. Reproducing the
    state's published figures, with the tests public, is the strongest available evidence that
    this site's arithmetic is right — and none of it is claimed until those tests exist and pass.
  </p>

  <h2>What the tool will not do</h2>
  <p>
    It does not score, rank or recommend scenarios, and there is no "savings" framing anywhere in
    it — not in the interface and not in the code, where the field is called a signed delta
    precisely so that nobody can quietly assume which direction was hoped for. Changes are shown
    in both directions with the assumptions that drive them visible and adjustable, and the
    assumptions register is published beside every result.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Errors get fixed in public. Every change to data, formulas or citations is a commit, and the
    <a href={`${base}/changelog/`}>changelog</a> renders them. When this site is wrong, the record
    of it being wrong and then corrected stays visible.
  </p>
</Base>
